An enum value that a variable holds, spelled by every target.

Go and JavaScript fell back to spelling an identifier of enum type as the
member of its name whenever the expression context did not resolve it, and a
function's parameters and locals are never in that context: `return r` came
out as `return "r"` and `held == other` as `"held" == "other"`. A member
reference carries `Ident.Member`, so a name is now a name.

Kotlin declared no class for a library enum a program stores, so
`ui.FontWeight.bold` named Compose's own `FontWeight`, which the file imports
and which has no `BOLD`. A library enum is declared as `SnglFontWeight`. And a
Kotlin entry printed as its upper-cased entry name; each now carries the SNGL
member name as its string form, as the member prints on every other target.

A library enum reaches Kotlin by more than a declaration: a list literal in a
loop head, a ternary Kotlin keeps as an expression, and the invocation list of
a slot a runtime instance declares each name one, and each has to find it
declared. The run record is what says so, the Kotlin compiler being the only
thing that notices an undeclared name. An enum with no members still needs the
`;` that ends its entry list, since the class declares a member after it.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `"(r|held|other)"` -- a parameter or local is its name, not a member called that
deny out/none/html/index.html `"(r|held|other)"` -- a parameter or local is its name, not a member called that
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `\bFontWeight\.[A-Z]+\b` -- a SNGL font weight is SnglFontWeight; bare FontWeight is Compose's
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `enum class Mood \{` -- an entry carries the member's declared name for its string form
-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

enum Mood { happy, sad }

enum Empty {}

struct Look {
    weight ui.FontWeight
}

func pick(r Mood) Mood {
    return r
}

func flip(r Mood) Mood {
    var held = r
    var other Mood = sad
    if held == other {
        return happy
    }
    return other
}

func shape(n int) string {
    var out = ""
    for var a = [ui.Justification.start, ui.Justification.end] {
        out = out + "{a}"
    }
    return out + "{n > 1 ? ui.Display.flex : ui.Display.block}"
}

component row(label component(a ui.TextAlign) ui.node) ui.node {
    var taps = 0
    ui.button(text="{taps}", @click { taps++ })
    label(ui.TextAlign.left)
}

ui.window {
    var mood = Mood.happy
    var w = ui.FontWeight.bold
    var look = Look{weight=ui.FontWeight.lighter}
    var none list<Empty> = []
    var open = false
    ui.button(text="next", @click {
        mood = flip(pick(mood))
        w = ui.FontWeight.normal
        open = !open
    })
    ui.text(value="{mood} {string(mood)} {w} {look.weight} {shape(none.length)}")
    if open {
        row() {
            component label(a) {
                ui.text(value="{a}")
            }
        }
    }
}
